<<~ ahu #/holds>>

! What each hearth HOLDS, in paths

The table above reads the ground in prose, which a hand reads and a gate cannot. This block names the same ground in ''repo-relative globs'', keyed by the trailer a hearth's commits carry, so `tools/hearths-gate.mjs` can refuse a commit that reaches into another hearth's ground before the ledger carries a crossing row for it. The prose stands as the meaning; the globs stand as its edge.

A glob that matches nothing belongs to no hearth — the gate names only what a commit actually touches. A path claimed by two hearths reads as a crossing in flight, and the gate says so rather than picking a winner.

''A closed hearth defends nothing, so its globs leave this fence.'' The fence states a ''claim against other hands'', and a session that has ended presses none. Leaving a closed hearth's globs here would refuse the only hearth left standing from ground nobody contests — an outage wearing a safeguard's clothes, which the gate's own header names as the shape it must not take. The ground the closed hearths held stands recorded twice over: in prose in the table above, and verbatim in the `holds-closed` fence below, which the gate does not parse.

!! What one hearth does to the gate

''Under a single hearth `tools/hearths-gate.mjs` refuses nothing, and it does so honestly rather than by being switched off.'' Its `verdict` refuses only a path that lands in a hold whose `trailer` differs from the committing hearth's; with one row, no path can. Every commit from `session_01Jg969FCpJcM84XvtsDNFou` reads back one line — ''N path(s) stand on `the founding hearth`'s ground'' — and a commit carrying no trailer (the operator's own) passes with its own note, as it always did.

''Leave the gate installed.'' It arms again the moment a second `[[hold]]` row appears — no code moves, no hook re-installs; the ledger IS the configuration, and the gate reads it fresh at every commit. Deleting the tool would make the next hearth's opening a build task instead of a table edit.

''The witness had to move, and the ledger says why.'' `tools/hearths-gate-witness.sh` drives the gate against ''the real ledger'' by design — a witness reading a fixture proves the parser rather than the rows the tree stands on. A one-hold ledger can produce no crossing, so its ★ RED vector would have passed by having nothing to refuse: a gate wearing a check it never earned. The witness now reads the live fence's count and, at one hold, runs its ARMING vectors against a fixture gathered from ''this ledger's own `[[hold]]` blocks, live and closed, in one live fence'' — the exact shape the tree takes when a second hearth opens — while every pass-through CONTROL keeps reading the real ledger. It PRINTS which ledger each half read. Nothing in `hearths-gate.mjs` moved.

⚠ ''One reading genuinely runs noisy under one hearth, and this hand names it rather than cutting it'': the pass note prints on ''every'' commit, so a hand watching hook output sees a line about hearth ground on work that crosses nothing. That reads as chatter, never as a lie — the note states a true fact about a tree with one hearth — so the noise wants the operator's word before a `--quiet` lands, since a gate that learns to say less is exactly the gate that stops being read.

```toml holds
[[hold]]
hearth  = "the founding hearth"
trailer = "session_01Jg969FCpJcM84XvtsDNFou"
globs   = [
  "packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts",
  "packages/lararium-node/scripts/build-genesis-island.ts",
  "packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts",
  "packages/lararium-node/src/genesis-artifact.ts",
